}\uc1\pard\qc\lang1033\f0\fs24 CHAPTER 1\par
\par
INTRODUCTION\par
\pard\par
The \i P?S\super\i0\f1  \nosupersub\i\f0 Enhanced PDP-8 Simulator\i0  (PEPS) is a software\f1 -based simulation\f0   of the 12-bit\f1  \i Digital Equipment Corporation\i0  (DEC) \f0 PDP-8\f1  \f0 computer\par
including \f1 most\f0  of \f1 the \f0 peripheral\f1  devices often available within a number of hardware configurations\f0 .\f1   Many of the PEPS package components were created by various members of the P?S\super [1]\nosupersub .\par
\par
\f0 The overall simulat\f1 ed environment\f0  is\f1  determined\f0  by specific initialization and\f1  \f0 configuration files \f1 controlling \f0 the basic "engine" of the \f1 PEPS package; this component is generally known as the \f0 SIMH\super\f1 [2]\nosupersub\f0  \f1 simulator \f0 for\f1  the \f0 PDP-8\f1  family of computers\f0 .\par
\par
\f1 This document serves as the primary User Guide to all features of the PEPS package.  Unless otherwise indicated, default PEPS configuration files are assumed to be in effect; this enables all PDP-8 devices\super [3]\nosupersub  required for either the entry-level paper-tape programs or any of several provided single-user storage-based operating systems as described in section 1.1 below.\par
\par
\f0 While the \f1 PEPS \f0 package can be used to simulate \f1 a \f0 minimal \f1 PDP-8\f0  \f1 environment\f0 , emphasis is given to larger systems that\f1  can support any of several provided single-user storage-based PDP-8 operating systems.  This allows execution of more complex programs, some of which are specific to the operating system environment chosen.\par
\par
If desired, new programs can be created using a variety of development techniques.  In the PEPS environment, program development focuses on using many facilities available in the host Microsoft Windows environment.  Depending on the user's needs, this can include creation of output files meant to be viewed in the host environment; in specific cases, various forms of post-processing can be applied for the purpose of creating output suitable for printing on Windows-compliant printers in a variety of output formats.  This could include creation of properly designed source code that yields fully formatted listing files of specific programs previously unavailable (other than perhaps in the form of primitive and/or badly copied paper listings with many limitations inhibiting any meaningful usage).\par
\par
\f0 This User Guide, together with the documents referenced in\f1  various appendices\f0 , provides information on the installation, maintenance and operation of the\f1  \f0 PEPS\f1  package\f0 .\par
\par
\f1 System Requirements and Initial Installation.\f0\par
\par
The PEPS package\f1  \f0 is designed to work on any release of Microsoft Windows XP, Windows Vista, Windows 7, Windows 8 or Windows 10.  It is generally distributed as a self-extracting archive capable of being installed on any permanently lettered logical drive.  Due to internal restrictions, the PEPS \f1 package \f0 cannot be located on network shares or removable drives where the logical drive letter \f1 is subject to\f0  change.\page Appendix B details the installation process\f1 , including the ability to create custom descendent installation packages should the user include any additional PEPS features beyond the standard release\f0 .\f1   The detailed instructions of the installation process must be closely followed in order to successfully create a working system.\par
\par
Logical Drive Considerations.\f0\par
\par
\f1 While not widely known, t\f0 he use of multiple \f1 logical \f0 drives in Windows is\f1  actually\f0  encouraged by Microsoft.  This is best accomplished using \f1 various\f0  third-party \f1 hard drive \f0 partitioning programs; however, certain Windows releases include Administrative Tools capable of \f1 performing a rudimentary form of partitioning that may be sufficient for the needs of many users.  Appendix C documents several third-party partitioning programs that are compatible with all systems that support the PEPS package.  These programs include additional features that may be of use to advanced users beyond the scope of logical drive placement of the PEPS package.\par
\par
\f0 Note: For illustrat\f1 ive\f0  purposes only, the PEPS package elements will be described throughout this document as if installed on \f1 Windows logical \f0 drive P\f1  (P:)\f0 .  Any permanently lettered logical device may be used\f1 ;\f0  \f1 h\f0 owever, it is recommended \f1 the \f0 PEPS\f1  package\f0  be installed on a dedicated drive to facilitate backup of working copies of the package.\par
\par
\f1 Additional Recommended Utilities beyond the PEPS Package.\f0\par
\par
Certain auxiliary programs compatible with Windows are optionally required\f1  depending on the user's needs; Appendix C includes\par
documentation for some of these additional applications.  Among these utilities are\f0  various text editors\f1  known to be compatible with the PEPS package\f0  and post-processing programs to facilitate viewing or printing text files created by PEPS \f1 (\f0 such as assembly listings of user programs\f1 )\f0 .\par
\par
Most of the PEPS documentation files\f1  (including this User Guide)\f0  are distributed in \i Portable Document Format\i0  \f1 (\f0 PDF\f1 ).  \f0 As such, a compatible document reading program \f1 (\f0 such as \i Adobe Reader\i0\f1 )\f0  \f1 should be installed prior to the first usage of the PEPS package\f0 .\par
\par
\f1 Note: It is recommended that all relevant considerations of sections mentioned above this point be reconciled before proceeding; the rest of this document assumes the PEPS package is already properly installed and ready to use; as the user gains familiarity with the PEPS package, minor changes can be introduced as necessary.\f0\par
\par
1.1\tab General Description\par
\par
The PEPS package provides a functional simulation of a PDP-8 system with various peripherals.  When compared to real-world hardware of various PDP-8 systems, the configuration is considered to be a large system. This provides much of the performance of the original PDP-8 systems while actually running on a Windows-based personal computer.\page As typically configured, the following \f1 PDP-8\f0  resources are generally available within the PEPS environment:\line\line a) PDP-8/E CPU\f1 ; this is \f0 generally known as\f1  the\f0  KK8F\f1  central processing\par
   unit \f0 as \f1 used\f0  in the PDP-8/E,\f1  \f0 PDP-8/F, PDP-8/M and the\f1  various\par
\f0  \f1   \f0 PDP-8/A-620\f1  \f0 series\f1  models\f0 .\par
\par
b) 32K of main memory.\par
\par
c) \i Extended Arithmetic Element\i0  \f1 (\f0 EAE\f1 )\f0 .  Note: Due to the presence of\par
   EAE, the corresponding PDP-8/A model is known as the PDP-8/A-625.\par
\par
\f1 Note: By adjusting certain parameters within the configuration files, the specific model of PDP-8 and availability of EAE can be changed to support certain earlier models.  Appendix Q contains more information on SIMH parameters which may be of interest to advanced users.\par
\line\f0 d) \f1 D\f0 evice 03/04 console terminal with full support of\f1  \f0 DEC standard\par
\f1  \f0  \f1  \f0 horizontal tab characters.  \f1 The use of a\f0 dvanced terminal-\par
\f1    \f0 handling software\f1  will correctly display\f0  \f1 console output with\par
   \f0 horizonta\f1 l \f0 tabs aligned\f1  to\f0  eight space\f1  multiples,\f0  as would\f1  \f0 be the\par
\f1   \f0  case on\f1  \f0 DEC\f1  \f0 terminals such as the VT100\f1 ; h\f0 owever, in \f1 many\f0  respects\par
\f1    \f0 the console\f1  \f0 display resembles the output\f1  \f0 of a Teletype Model 33\par
\f1    printing unit\f0  using pale\f1  \f0 yellow roll paper and bold black\f1  \f0 ink\par
\f1   \f0  ribbon.\par
\par
   Note: For operating systems\f1  \f0 with known defects in\f1  \f0 console terminal\par
\f1   \f0  handling\f1  \f0 such as OS/8\super [\f1 4\f0 ]\nosupersub , the console simulation can be\f1  \f0 restricted\par
 \f1   \f0 forcing upper-case input and output, as well as\f1  \f0 suppression of\par
 \f1   \f0 unintended output of certain control characters.\par
\par
e) Device 66 standard line printer \f1 (\f0 such as LP08\f1 )\f0 .  Note: For certain\par
   configurations this can be deliberately disabled as necessary.\par
\par
f) PC04 High-Speed Reader \f1 (\f0 device 01\f1 )\f0  and Punch \f1 (\f0 device 02\f1 )\f0 .  Note:\par
   Individual file transfer from the \f1 PEPS host environment\f0  into the\par
\f1    \f0 SIMH\f1  \f0 environment can only be achieved using the high-speed reader.\par
   Individual file transfer from the SIMH environment to the\f1  PEPS\f0\par
   host\f1  environment\f0  can be accomplished using either the device 66\par
 \f1   \f0 line printer or\f1  \f0 the high-speed punch.\par
\par
g) TC08 \f1 (\f0 or TC01\f1 )\f0  DECtape Controller with up to eight DECtape drive\par
   units \f1 numbered \f0 0\f1  through \f0 7.  Note: Each logical drive must be\par
 \f1   \f0 configured by being\f1  \f0 associated with an active compatible device\par
 \f1   \f0 file that represents\f1  \f0 the contents of the DECtape mounted on the\par
 \f1   \f0 particular drive.  If\f1  \f0 the associated file doesn't exist, it will\par
\f1   \f0  automatically be created\f1  \f0 when attempting to write to the drive.\par
 \f1  \f0  Any non-configured drive\f1  \f0 will return a SELECT error if read access\par
 \f1   \f0 is attempted.\par
\par
h) Primary \f1 (\f0 device 77\f1 )\f0  TD8E Controller with up to two DECtape drives\par
   with the same file considerations as in g) above.  Note: The TD8E\par
   controller and the TC08 controller are mutually exclusive.\page i) RK8E \f1 (\f0 device 74\f1 )\f0  Cartridge Disk Controller with up to four RK05\par
   drives.  As in the case of DECtape drives, should the associated\par
\f1    drive file not exist, it will automatically be created.\par
\par
j) Primary (device 75) RX8E Controller with up to two RX01 drives.  As\par
   in the case of RK05 drives, should the associated drive file not\par
   exist, it will automatically be created.\par
\par
Additional devices are available depending on the specific configuration of the SIMH simulator.  Appendix D refers to these devices; however, specific software drivers or additional utility programs may be required for functional support.\par
\par
Note: Limitations of the SIMH environment prevent dismountable devices such as DECtapes, RK05 and RX01 drives from being loaded or unloaded during SIMH sessions; a design goal of the \f0 PEPS package\f1  is to allow advanced users the ability to rapidly modify the specific configuration followed by a restart of the SIMH simulation process to mitigate these issues.  Wherever possible, the various components of the SIMH simulator are maintained in separate directories to avoid needless confusion.  Special emphasis is given to accessing the configuration files that determine the specific simulation.  Any number of configurations can be available on a given system; however only one configuration can be run at any given time.  This allows maximum flexibility in terms of mounted device specifics and operating system configurations accessible from a centralized directory.\par
\par
1.2  General Usage\par
\par
Note: PDP-8 software supports several different file structures, all referred to in the 12-bit environment as \i directories\i0 .  In Windows, the analogous structures are often referred to as \i folders\i0 .  Within this User Guide, the terms will be used interchangeably only when referring to the file structures of Microsoft Windows.\par
\par
Using the PEPS package typically starts by calling up the PEPS Command folder to the Windows desktop; this serves as the "command center" for most PEPS operations.  From this folder, nearly all PEPS functions and utilities can be initiated.  This includes bringing up the SIMH PDP-8 simulator booting to one of several supported operating system configurations, various file-related functions such as file editing and file maintenance in the host Windows environment.\par
\par
The user must have a general understanding of Microsoft Windows to make use of many of the available features of the \f0 PEPS package\f1 . Knowledge of the PDP-8 operating systems is essential to use the various facilities provided and, if desired, help to draw comparisons between the supported operating systems.  Appendix A includes references to documents for the various supported PDP-8 operating systems which can aid in their usage, many of which are provided in the full PEPS distribution.\par
\par
The Command directory is described in greater detail in section 1.50 below.\page Note: The PEPS package includes desktop icons/shortcuts for the\par
Command directory itself and also the underlying folder group that supports it.  All users generally start in the Command directory to initiate nearly all operations while the underlying folder group is meant to be accessed by either advanced users or actual developers of the PEPS package.  It is strongly recommended that both desktop shortcuts be installed on all systems; this will allow a smooth transition as the user gains familiarity with the full complement of implemented features (including available customization techniques).\par
\par
1.3\tab Supported PDP-8 Operating Systems\par
\par
As of this writing, various configurations of two different PDP-8 operating systems are supported.\par
\par
1) OS/8 Version V3D (V3Q)\par
