\par
1.64 Special-purpose utility commands\par
\par
\pard\sl240\slmult1\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, all special-purpose utility commands \tab are located in the fourth row of the Command directory window.\par
\par
\tab All of the commands in this section are permanent components of\par
\tab the PEPS package concerned with the files used to \tab intercommunicate between the host Windows environment and the \tab SIMH simulated environment; these files are located in the \i\\PDP-8\par
\tab\\SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory.  The commands are used to carry\par
\tab out various functions on these files needed when using any of the\par
\tab supported operating systems.  The specific files are described \tab below:\par
\par
\tab 1) LPTOUT.TXT - This file is used to capture the output directed\par
\tab    to the device 66 line-printer.  Various commands are\par
\tab    implemented to manage the contents of this file.\page\tab 2) PTPOUT.TXT - This file is used to capture the output directed \tab    to the high-speed punch [PTP:] device.  While the file\par
\tab    extension used might imply printable text contents, the actual\par
\tab    file contents could be non-printable binary coding depending\par
\tab    on specific circumstances.  Actual text contents can be opened\par
\tab    in any designated editing program capable of processing ASCII\par
\tab    text characters in the file.  Windows Notepad is the default\par
\tab    program used throughout the PEPS package for such editing\par
\tab    purposes.  On an case by case basis, advanced users can modify\par
\tab    the underlying command files to use a qualified alternate\par
\tab    text editing program if desired.\par
\par
 \tab 3) PTRIN.TXT - This file is used to transmit input data to the\par
\tab    high-speed reader [PTR:] device.  As described in 2) above,\par
\tab    the file extension might imply printable text contents.\par
\tab    However, the actual file contents could be binary data such as\par
\tab    a standard PDP-8 binary file in \i BIN\super [4]\nosupersub\i0  format.  Generally the\par
\tab    user determines the specific contents of the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file\par
\tab    and the set of utilities provided within the designated\par
\tab    operating system that will process the file as intended.\par
\par
\tab The various commands described in this section manage nearly all\par
\tab situations related to these key files.  In certain rare \tab instances, it may become necessary to use one of the \tab miscellaneous commands described \tab below in section 1.65 to\par
\tab accomplish certain tasks using Windows Explorer.  This would \tab generally concern transfer of files obtained via the Internet or\par
\tab other external resources.  Appendix Q contains more information\par
\tab on optional commands to be included in this section or currently\par
 \tab under development.  If specific utilities are proposed and \tab implemented, command links can be added to this section in future\par
\tab releases.  \par
\par
\tab a) Clear LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file is emptied.  This\par
\tab    command is needed to ensure there are no residual characters\par
\tab    in the file resulting from previous SIMH sessions.  If the\par
\tab    purpose of the next SIMH session is to produce a listing file\par
\tab    directed to the device 66 LPT: device, this command must\par
\tab    precede launching the SIMH simulator.\par
\par
\tab b) Strip LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file often contains known\par
\tab    artifacts created by imperfect operation of various output\par
\tab    utilities in the supported operating systems.  Left as created\par
\tab    by the issuing utility, the file is often useless in the\par
\tab    Windows environment.\page\tab    Some of the problems with undesired \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file contents\par
\tab    arise from necessary implementation restrictions within the\par
\tab    original real-world PDP-8 environment; certain utilities\par
\tab    time out the LPT: device and then fall back on the console\par
\tab    printer should the LPT: not be available.  In some instances,\par
\tab    these characters would have been ignored in the sense that no\par
\tab    artifacts would appear on the printed page of a real-world\par
\tab    system line printer; however, the SIMH simulator captures all\par
\tab    characters sent to the LPT:.  As such, it is generally\par
\tab    necessary to remove these unwanted characters from the\par
\tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file.  In certain situations, the artifact\par
\tab    characters, while present, are truly unfathomable or only work\par
\tab    with specific printer models yet were never discovered during\par
\tab    the economic lifetime of these operating systems.  In the case\par
\tab    of certain P?S/8 line printer quirks, the original development\par
\tab    environment often lacked access to a line printer and the\par
\tab    program implemented the best guesses of the programmer.  Some\par
\tab    of these issues resulted in wasting a sheet of line printer\par
\tab    paper; the intended output started on the next page at the top\par
\tab    of form point.\par
\par
\tab    To solve all of the problems discussed above [and arcane\par
\tab    additional issues not documented here], the PEPS package\par
\tab    includes the \i STRIP.TEC\i0  macro designed to work with \i TECOC\i0 , a\par
\tab    freeware implementation of DEC standard \i TECO\i0  for Windows.\par
\tab    Once a file is processed  by \i STRIP.TEC\i0 , all such undesired\par
\tab    output is eliminated; the resultant file is now ready for\par
\tab    further processing such as input to a word-processor for the\par
\tab    purpose of printing the file in Windows.  In some cases, post-\par
\tab    processing might include conversion to PDF format for\par
\tab    distribution to a wider audience or file archiving.\par
\par
\tab    Running the \ul\i STRIP.TEC\ulnone\i0  macro on typical text files is a time-\tab\par
\tab    consuming process; the actual execution time depends on the\par
\tab    Windows system processing power, \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file length and\par
\tab    other considerations.  As shown in Figure 11, a separate\par
\tab    window is created to display the stripping process; the\par
\tab    starting and ending times are displayed during the program\par
\tab    execution to aid the user in determining future processing\par
\tab    time should the file [or a subsequent version after a source\par
\tab    file edit] require additional stripping.\par
\par
\tab    While the example shown in Figure 11 took just over 90 seconds\par
\tab    to complete, it is not uncommon for very long assembly\par
\tab    listings to take over five minutes for conversion into a\par
\tab    properly formatted file on typical Windows systems.\page\pard\sl240\slmult1\qc\par
