   Figure 11. LPTOUT.TXT file stripping session\par
\par
\tab c) Open LPT Output.  The \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file is opened in the\par
\tab    standard Windows Notepad editor.  This allows immediate\par
\tab    display of the consequences of processing such as assembling\par
\tab    the source file currently under development.\par
\par
\tab    Note: While there is generally no need to modify the\par
 \tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file, editing operations are freely allowed; the\par
\tab    file may be printed if desired.  In many circumstances, the\par
\tab    font size must be reduced to obtain a complete display on the\par
\tab    Windows desktop without resorting to horizontal scrolling.\par
\tab    This typically occurs because assembly language utilities add\par
\tab    additional content at the left margin of every source file\par
\tab    line.\par
\par
\tab    While alternative editors may be useful for typical editing\par
\tab    situations, most other qualifying editing utilities may either\par
\tab    cause all open text files to share a common window [by\par
\tab    selecting one of a set of multiple tabs] or not support\par
\tab    multiple font sizes in a single session.  Unless the\par
\tab    alternate program can support these additional features, it is\par
\tab    recommended that Windows Notepad continue to be used for this\par
\tab    purpose.\par
\par
\tab d) Clear PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is emptied.  This\par
\tab    command is needed to ensure there are no residual characters\par
\tab    in the file resulting from previous SIMH sessions.  If the\par
\tab    purpose of the next SIMH session is to produce a text file\par
\tab    directed to the PTP: device, this command must precede\par
\tab    launching the SIMH simulator.\par
\par
\tab e) Strip PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is stripped of unwanted\par
\tab    artifacts; other than the change of target file from\par
 \tab    \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  to \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0 , the entire process as described in\par
\tab    b) above applies to this command.\page\tab    It has been occasionally observed that the PTPOUT.TXT file\par
\tab    will contain a trailing form-feed character after the file has\par
\tab    been stripped of all other artifacts.  The root cause of this\par
\tab    minor issue has been determined to be an obscure quirk of the\par
\tab    OS/8 PTP: handler due to an intrinsic design problem within\par
\tab    the handler.  Notably, if the identical file is processed by\par
\tab    the OS/8 LPT: handler [into the \i LPTOUT.TXT\i0  file] the problem\par
\tab    doesn't occur.\par
\par
\tab    Should this situation be problematic, the character can be\par
\tab    removed manually using the \i Open PTP Output\i0  command as\par
\tab    described in f) below; alternatively, the entire stripping\par
\tab    process can be repeated to eliminate the unwanted artifact.\par
\par
\tab    Due to the manner in which the OS/8 PTP: handler functions,\par
\tab    while the form-feed character is the last such character in\par
\tab    the original file, it is not actually the sentinel character\par
\tab    of the file, generally surrounded by sequences of unwanted\par
\tab    characters requiring removal at a different stage of the\par
\tab    stripping process.  Future releases of the STRIP.TEC macro may\par
\tab    be able to remove this defect in more instances than\par
\tab    presently.  [As of the current release of the stripping\par
\tab    program, this artifact is already being removed in many cases,\par
\tab    albeit apparently not in every case.]\par
\par
\tab f) Open PTP Output.  The \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is opened in the\par
\tab    standard Windows Notepad editor.  This allows immediate\par
\tab    display of the consequences of processing such as assembling\par
\tab    the source file currently under development.\par
\par
\tab    Other than the change of target file from LPTOUT.TXT to\par
\tab    PTPOUT.TXT, the entire process as described in c) above\par
\tab    applies to this command.\par
\par
\tab g) Swap PTR and PTP Files.  The contents of the \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  [PTR:]\par
\tab    and \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  [PTP:] files are interchanged.  This provides\par
 \tab    file interchange useful in specific circumstances.\par
\par
\tab    For example, binary files created in P?S/8 can be made\par
\tab    available for OS/8 usage [or vice-versa] using this command.\par
\tab    This allows OS/8 users to take advantage of the unique\par
\tab    features of P?S/8 \i PAL\i0  which supports additional functionality\par
\tab    beyond OS/8 PAL8 while maintaining total PAL language\par
\tab    compatibility.\par
\par
\tab    With the availability of the P?S/8 \i PTUTIL\i0  program, text files\par
\tab    may be directly interchanged between these operating systems  \tab    without involving the host Windows environment.  Note: Other\par
 \tab    methods are available for file interchange between these two\par
\tab    operating systems.  Appendix V describes a variety of methods,\par
\tab    some of which are currently under development.\page\pard 1.65 Miscellaneous commands\par
\par
\tab Note: As shown in Figure 3, all miscellaneous commands are\par
\tab located in the bottom row of the Command directory window.\par
\par
\tab a) SIMH Working Files.  The \i SIMH_Working_Files\i0  directory is\par
\tab    opened in a separate window.  Using Windows Explorer\par
\tab    techniques, various files may be accessed for such purposes as\par
\tab    file interchange with external sources.\par
\par
\tab    This folder is also the working directory for device image\par
\tab    files.  By copying device image files here [and editing the\par
\tab    appropriate SIMH configuration files] the contents of device\par
\tab    image files can be made active in the SIMH environment.\par
\tab    Appendix Y details the important files located in this\par
 \tab    directory.\par
\par
\tab    Note: Normal activity of the various components of the PEPS\par
\tab    package will tend to produce temporary files in this\par
\tab    directory; in extreme cases, it may be necessary to manually \par
\tab    remove temporary files.  However, the majority of temporary\par
 \tab    filenames are internally recycled, largely avoiding any\par
        maintenance requirements.\par
\par
\tab b) Help Documents.  The \i SIMH Documents\i0  directory is opened in a\par
\tab    separate window, allowing access to a variety of documentation\par
\tab    files on various PEPS-related subjects.  Since most of the\par
\tab    files are in .PDF format, a suitable program such as \i Adobe\par
\tab    Reader\i0  must be installed.  Appendix U provides additional\par
\tab    information on the available documents.\par
\par
\tab c) Inactive Programs.  The \i Inactive Programs\i0  directory is opened\par
\tab    in a separate window.  Generally, icon/link files are stored\par
\tab    here for programming projects not currently under development.\par
\tab    Several of these files are provided by advanced users of the\par
\tab    PEPS package for the purpose of developing their own PDP-8\par
\tab    programs.  All of the files are useful as templates for\par
\tab    creating additional personal program development commands.\par
\par
\tab    Note: The provided files in the \i Inactive Programs\i0  directory\par
\tab    will not be actionable without the underlying support files.\par
\tab    In some cases, the referenced source files are proprietary and\par
\tab    are not available with the standard release of the PEPS\par
\tab    package.  However, the mechanism to exercise the icon/link\par
\tab    files should be functional because dummy files are provided in\par
\tab    lieu of the original source files.\par
\par
\tab d) SIMH Config Files.  The \i SIMH Configuration Files\i0  directory is\par
\tab    opened in a separate window.  Within this directory are the\par
\tab    configuration files used with each variant operating system\par
\tab    used with the SIMH simulator.  Changes made to these files can\par
\tab    greatly affect the way the corresponding operating systems\par
 \tab    function.\page Notes and References.\par
\par
 1. The \i Poly Question Society\i0  (abbreviated wherever possible as P?S,\par
    or PQS if only a limited character set is available and pronounced\par
    as if spelled \ul pee-kew-ess\ulnone ) is an informal group of questioners\par
    concerned with a multitude of subjects.  In general, the members\par
    of the P?S (whether alive, dead or imaginary) met at an\par
    institution of higher learning known as the \i Polytechnic Institute\par
    of Brooklyn \i0 or more informally "Brooklyn Poly" (now a division of\par
    \i New York University\i0 ).  Nearly all P?S members are computer\par
    programmers known for exceptional works implemented on various\par
    computer architectures, especially the DEC PDP-8 family.\par
\par
    The P?S is an outgrowth of a pre-existing organization known as\par
    the \i William Claude Fields Memorial Programming Group\i0  (WCFMPG)\par
    (also known as \i Friends of the Boston Massacre)\i0 .  The various works\par
    of the WCFMPG are legendary in the realm of PDP-8 programming; all\par
    original WCFMPG members were responsible for either starting or\par
    creating various software projects for DEC computers, especially\par
    the PDP-8.  All members of the WCFMPG were DEC employees at some\par
    point in their careers; however, much of their work on PDP-8\par
    systems (and other computers made by other companies) predates\par
    their employment at Digital.\par
\par
 2. SIMH stands for "history simulation" (in the sense of the history\par
    of computing) for the purpose of preserving as much as possible\par
    the functionality of computers generally no longer manufactured.\par
    The SIMH project is maintained by a loose affiliation of\par
    programmers, most generally specializing in one or two computer\par
    architectures out of the many systems supported.\par
\par
    Levels of support for specific computers varies; at one point the\par
    SIMH project was very DEC-centric but now includes support for\par
    models from various other manufacturers.\par
\par
    \f0 More information on the\f1  \f0 SIMH project can\f1  \f0 be\f1  \f0 obtained from various\par
 \f1    \f0 Internet websites\f1  hosting components of the overall SIMH project\par
    including various source code files and other forms of\par
    documentation.\par
\par
 3. SIMH simulator support is available for additional PDP-8 features\par
    and devices normally disabled by default; however, additional\par
    options are documented within the SIMH environment by invoking\par
    certain internal \i help\i0  commands.  Where relevant, additional\par
    documentation is provided in Appendix A above.  For those\par
    \f0 seeking \f1 further\f0  \f1 information\f0 , links to\f1  certain Internet\f0  websites\par
\f1     \f0 are provided\f1  \f0 that may supply information\f1  regarding seldom used\par
    features of SIMH; h\f0 owever, most users of PEPS will not be\par
\f1     \f0 concerned unless intending to develop software\f1  expressly t\f0 arget\f1 ing\par
\f0  \f1    specific \f0 devices.\f1\page  4. OS/8 is the principal marketing name of an operating system that\par
    can run on most PDP-8 models (or in some instances a specific\par
    variant name is used because of the need to support slightly\par
    different and likely incompatible models which cannot run the main\par
    system components).  The group of name variant systems constitutes\par
    a confusing mess due to some of these variations being created\par
    merely for the purposes of highly dubious marketing ploys; only\par
    an expert can tell the difference between a variant with little or\par
    no technical purpose over OS/8 Version 3D (also known as Version\par
    3Q) and another slightly different system that can only be used\par
    with newer models such as what are known as Omnibus machines, or\par
    perhaps DECmates depending on the specific variations, albeit not\par
    necessarily completely functional).  In general it is prudent to\par
    avoid these newer variants when using the SIMH simulator due to\par
    the likelihood of subtle incompatibilities suddenly appearing\par
    seemingly without warning.\par
\par
    Certain specific programs acquired from newer release variants as\par
    mentioned above provide acceptable functionality when used in\par
    OS/8 V3D.  As such, the image copy of OS/8 provided in the PEPS\par
    package contains several notable programs that correct bugs and\par
    quirks in the originally released versions associated with OS/8\par
    Version V3D.\par
\par
    OS/8 was originally created as a quick-and-dirty hack pulled\par
    off over a single weekend intended as a demo program to be shown\par
    to DEC management the next Monday morning.  While there was every\par
    intention to write a second system with more reasoned design\par
    internals, foolishly, management insisted all further changes be\par
    made to the demo version instead of a complete rewrite.  As a\par
    direct result,  many serious problems became institutionalized\par
    into the design that cannot be corrected.\par
\par
    The original demo (and some of the follow-on work) was done by\par
    Richard Lary, who also created most of the R-L Monitor System\par
    (also known later as \i MS/8\i0 ) along with the rest of the members of\par
    the WCFMPG.  The R-L Monitor System was the first PDP-8 operating\par
    system ever used by most of the members of the P?S.\par
\par
    Part of the reason the deficient demo was created is because the\par
    R-L Monitor System was shown to DEC management personnel, all of\par
    whom rejected it essentially because they had no idea what it was\par
    or what it actually did; the demo was designed to show how a\par
    computer user could approach the PDP-8 in a manner vaguely similar\par
    to the way this was done on other DEC computers available at the\par
    time such as using TOPS10 on the PDP-10 from the point of view of\par
    a single user.  As such, the managers obtained a completely\par
    superficial and flawed understanding of the demo (which for them\par
    was likely the most they could muster considering their minimal\par
    understanding of the other operating systems mentioned above that\par
    were in place at the time).\page     It is noteworthy that all of the other members of the WCFMPG\par
    refused to work on the demo because of its flimsy nature.  Later,\par
    some other members of the P?S were hired by DEC to work on OS/8\par
    along with a few unrelated staffers of varying abilities.  While\par
    this mixed group did eventually develop the demo into something\par
    more functional, the inherent internal limitations cannot be\par
    overcome without a complete rewrite (which had been proposed but\par
    was rejected by management).\par
\par
    Using OS/8 today, whether on real-world hardware or within the\par
    PEPS environment or other simulators, requires mastering certain\par
    unusual skills on the part of the user such as working in a\par
    mythical year environment sometime prior to 01-Jan-2002.\par
    Unfortunately, due to limitations within the file system, it is\par
    not possible to determine the proper year of any particular file\par
    because there is an inherent anomaly regarding which group of\par
    eight years in the range of 1970-1977, 1978-1985, 1986-1993 or\par
    1994-2001 is the true file origination date reported by the\par
    available utilities in an obfuscated manner.\par
\par
    In specific instances, particular users know the proper year group\par
    for certain files from personal experience, but inevitably, any\par
    collection of files will become commingled with files from one of\par
    the other three possible date groups; worse still, program files\par
    created today must have mythical dates.  New users must keep track\par
    of the real-world date offset on a file by file basis.  Because of\par
    this serious anomaly, coupled with  the advantages offered by\par
    advanced features of the PEPS package, it is strongly recommended\par
    that all users embrace host editing of source files.  Within the\par
    host environment, more descriptive filenames are possible with\par
    accurate file dates as long as the file date can be set after\par
    01-Jan-1980.  (Note: This still causes problems identifying files\par
    known to have been created from 01-Jan-1970 through 31-Dec-1979 or\par
    perhaps earlier.)\par
\par
    Certain intrepid users are known to manually insert pragmatic date\par
    modifications in the output files of OS/8 directory listings to\par
    maintain fantasy records that may contain more information than\par
    OS/8 can actually provide.  However, due to Y2K considerations,\par
    this practice has an upper limit of 31-Dec-2069.\par
\par
 5. P?S/8 is a replacement system for the R-L Monitor system.  It \par
    includes thousands of improvements borne of all-new work carried\par
    out over the course of decades.  Despite all of this, the basic\par
    P?S/8 system is quite low overhead (or in some subtle ways\par
    actually lower overhead), when compared to the original R-L\par
    Monitor System.  On many specific hardware configurations, P?S/8\par
    runs palpably faster than OS/8 performing comparable tasks.\par
\par
    Certain specific concepts unique to the R-L Monitor System (and\par
    not translatable to OS/8) have either been faithfully implemented\par
    in P?S/8, or in some cases, actually improved.\page     P?S/8 is still being developed.  While the P?S/8 release included\par
    in the current PEPS package is Version 8Z first released on\par
    23-Feb-1988, various improvements and bug fixes have been applied\par
    to the system image included in the PEPS release.\par
\par
    As soon as P?S/8 Version 9A is completed, it will replace the\par
    present Version 8Z release and be included in future PEPS\par
    packages.\par
\par
    P?S/8 runs on all of the same PDP-8 models that OS/8 (and its\par
    variants) runs on; however, the single P?S/8 release covers all\par
    models, differing only in terms of specific device handlers and\par
    related utilities where applicable.  It is noteworthy that a P?S/8\par
    system written to RX01 diskette media can be booted to all\par
    suitably configured models from the original "straight" PDP-8\par
    through the DECmate II.  By way of comparison, three different\par
    OS/8 variants are required to support the same models due to\par
    insurmountable incompatibilities inherent in OS/8 internal design\par
    (and at least four variants were marketed for this purpose).\par
    Note: No OS/8 variant exists to support bootable RX01 diskettes on\par
    the DECmate II.\par
\par
    A minor patch must be applied to the supplied P?S/8 release for\par
    the TC01/TC08 DECtape controller due to SIMH incompatibility with\par
    the real-world hardware; the P?S/8 keyboard monitor includes\par
    direct commands to rewind and unload DECtapes.  When applied on\par
    the real-world hardware, appropriate use of these commands helps\par
    achieve noticeable performance improvements; unfortunately, using\par
    these commands is known to destabilize the SIMH program.\par
\par
    Documentation is available explaining the minor (reversible)\par
    patching procedure needed if moving the system image to an actual\par
    DECtape for use on real-world systems.  Various programs exist to\par
    move DECtape images between physical DECtapes and the\par
    SIMH-compatible files used in the PEPS environment.  Appendix D\par
    provides links to appropriate utilities for this and related\par
    purposes.\par
\par
 6. BIN format is the standard binary loading format defined in the\par
    paper-tape software system.  On occasion, a BIN format file must\par
    be loaded into memory in the SIMH simulated environment.  Both the\par
    OS/8 and P?S/8 operating systems support utilities to process BIN\par
    format data passed to the SIMH environment by setting up the\par
    \i PTRIN.TXT\i0  file with the intended binary contents.  When files are\par
    moved from the SIMH environment to the host Windows system, the\par
    \i PTPOUT.TXT\i0  file is used.\page ____________________________________________________________________\par
